{
  "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
  "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nMICHAEL R. DREEBEN, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nBENJAMIN SNYDER, Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; for the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nKANNON K. SHANMUGAM, ESQUIRE, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DREEBEN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nThis case involves a fundamental question about the meaning of content-based regulation under the First Amendment. The Fifth Circuit interpreted this Court's decision in Reed to mean that any time that an officer must read a sign to apply the law, the law is content-based.\nThat holding is wrong and should be reversed. A law is content-based on its face when the text of the law singles out specific subject matter for differential treatment. The law in Reed did that by distinguishing ideological, political, and directional signs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0000-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It cited Sorrell, Carey, and Mosley. Sorrell is a case about the restriction of dissemination of pharmaceutical-related information. Mosley and Carey both involve picketing ordinances that singled out labor picketing as subjects that were permitted and all other picketing was banned. That provides a frame of reference for what the Court meant when it said in Reed itself that laws targeting specific subject matter are content-based."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What -- what if it says \"no signs within 25 yards of the highway, except for signs advertising a business in Austin?\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So, if I understand it correctly, if it's content-based, you agree that strict scrutiny applies and you are not making an argument that you could prevail on strict scrutiny, presumably, because you don't think you have a sufficiently compelling interest under this Court's precedents. But, if it's content neutral, you say intermediate scrutiny applies and that you win because you have a sufficiently important or significant government interest, even though not compelling. Is that correct so far?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAVANAUGH",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My question is, how do we -- how does that historical practice and the commonality of the restrictions and the precedent affect whether we decide the threshold question of content-based or content neutrality?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The court of appeals held that a sign ordinance that distinguishes between on-premises signs and off-premises signs is just as suspicious as an ordinance that distinguishes between Democratic signs and Republican signs or between religious signs and secular signs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice THOMAS",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In your briefs -- brief, you recommended that we apply the Secondary Effects Doctrine?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice THOMAS",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Has this Court applied that doctrine outside of the adult entertainment business cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0011",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "this case deals with a category of speech that doesn't have any inherent content. And so, if -- if you want to think about how to sort of recognize that as a -- a -- a separate category that we're not going to treat as content-based without watering down strict scrutiny, I think the sorts of interests that the Court looked to in City of Renton in terms of deciding that a law that, you know, you could plausibly say was content-based on its face would nevertheless be treated as content-neutral."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And so why doesn't that have a knock-on effect on content?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GORSUCH",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0008",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that point, but doesn't it necessarily favor majoritarian voices? Wouldn't you agree with that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "International Sign Association",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0001-0012",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you look at the amicus brief of the International Sign Association, it talks a little about -- a little bit about the experience in Chicago. And Chicago -- Chicago's experience was that they did away with the on-premises/off-premises distinction and went to a rule about allowing signs up to a hundred square feet, without regard to on-premises or off-premises, and those signs proliferated throughout the city."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the -- which we think is the appropriate framework here, then Austin is not required to adopt much less effective regulations of signs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Snyder",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SNYDER: I think that's right, Your Honor. I -- and I think that's why the Court has not embraced a disparate impact theory of the First Amendment and why it would be a mistake to do so here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: One --one question. If you're concerned about safety and blight, which are the two concerns that the City has articulated, the question we have to ask is whether that -- those interests could be served in ways that wouldn't draw a distinction based on content or wouldn't infringe speech generally, whether you could serve the same interests."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The City of Austin denied Respondents' application to convert its existing signs to digital signs, and it did so on the ground that the signs advertised off-premises activities. Under this Court's decision in Reed, Austin's distinction between signs advertising on-premises and off-premises activities is content-based."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Counsel, why wouldn't we analyze this under Commercial Speech Doctrine?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Well, this is conduct too, conduct of having an off-site grandfathered billboard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 78,
        "start": 78
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: Sorry to interrupt, but to stop you there, I think there was confusion after Reed about on-premises/off-premises because it was unclear where a majority of the Court was in the wake of the different opinions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0004-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 88,
        "start": 88
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Suppose a -- a city has two categories of sign regulations. One is for signs that are in front of a building. The other is for signs that are not in front of a building. And it says that signs in the first category may not exceed a certain size. Signs in the second category may not exceed a smaller size. Is that content -based?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0004-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 89,
        "start": 89
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What is the difference between that and what happened here? You have certain signs -- I'll come back to my question. I still -- my first question, I still don't quite understand the -- the answer. You have certain signs. Austin doesn't say you have to take them down. It just says you can't digitize them. And that isn't a content-based distinction between a digitized sign and a non-digitized sign. Maybe it's not a defensible distinction, but it doesn't seem to be content-based."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0004-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 90,
        "start": 90
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Shanmugam, I --I mean, I guess the question is, yes, you can say that there's a piece of content that triggers the restriction. It has to advertise off-premises activities."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-20-1029",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "68db19da18c0deb426b5288627bd6b5fb7e64cca03efba2b2840995eaafb6744",
      "evidence_id": "ev-68db19da18c0-0004-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 102,
        "start": 102
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
